<<<
foist. The general public is much too easily foisted off with the old cry of the shopman that ‘ there’s no demand for that kind of thing’. The public can be fobbed off with something, or the something can be fobbed off on the public; but foist has only the second construction; see ANALOGY & OBJECT-SHUFFLING.
===
**foist.** *The general public is much too easily* foisted off *with the old cry of the shopman that ' there's no demand for that kind of thing'.* The public can be *fobbed* off with something, or the something can be *fobbed* off on the public; but *foist* has only the second construction; see ^^ANALOGY^^ & ^^OBJECT-SHUFFLING^^.
>>>

<<<
folio. Pl. -os; see -o(£)s 4. The following account from a dictionary may be useful:—Leaf of paper &c. numbered only on front; two opposite pages, or single page, of ledger used for the two sides of account; number of words (72 or 90) as unit of length in document; (Bookbinding) once-folded sheet of printing-paper giving two leaves or four pages (in f., made of ff.), (also f. volume &c.) a book or volume in f., (similarly of smaller sheets & books resulting from various foldings & named after the number of leaves to the sheet: quarto or 4to, folded twice into four leaves; sexto or 6to, thrice into 6
===
**folio.** Pl. *-os*; see ^^-O(E)S^^ 4. The following account from a dictionary may be useful:—Leaf of paper &c. numbered only on front; two opposite pages, or single page, of ledger used for the two sides of account; number of words (72 or 90) as unit of length in document; (Bookbinding) once-folded sheet of printing-paper giving two leaves or four pages (*in f.,* made of ff.), (also *f. volume* &c.) a book or volume in f., (similarly of smaller sheets & books resulting from various foldings & named after the number of leaves to the sheet: *quarto* or *4to*, folded twice into four leaves; *sexto* or *6to*, thrice into 6
>>>
